                },
                {
                  "type": "frame",
                  "id": "eZFSj",
                  "name": "Distance law",
                  "width": 820,
                  "layout": "vertical",
                  "gap": 12,
                  "children": [
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "id": "zqpZc",
                      "name": "Heading",
                      "fill": "$ink",
                      "content": "2. Blocks and target distances",
                      "fontFamily": "$font",
                      "fontSize": 18,
                      "fontWeight": "700"
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "id": "a4eV7",
                      "name": "P",
                      "fill": "#3F3E3B",
                      "textGrowth": "fixed-width",
                      "width": "fill_container",
                      "content": "Overlapping loops form a block, and the region is split into blocks at points that no loop spans. Stretches without loops have no beads. Every pair of anchors then gets a target distance from one law in bead units, one bead being the distance at 1 kb. A pair without a loop has the target b(s), with s the genomic separation. A loop of strength q has the target d(s, q).",
                      "lineHeight": 1.6,
                      "fontFamily": "$font",
                      "fontSize": 14,
                      "fontWeight": "normal"
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "frame",
                      "id": "j3QLK",
                      "name": "Formulas",
                      "width": "fill_container",
                      "fill": "$panel",
                      "cornerRadius": 8,
                      "layout": "vertical",
                      "gap": 6,
                      "padding": [
                        12,
                        16
                      ],
                      "children": [
                        {
                          "type": "text",
                          "id": "R9ZPw",
                          "name": "F1",
                          "fill": "$ink",
                          "content": "b(s) = max(1, (s / 1 kb) ^ nu)",
                          "fontFamily": "$mono",
                          "fontSize": 13,
                          "fontWeight": "normal"
                        },
                        {
                          "type": "text",
                          "id": "jJRD0",
                          "name": "F2",
                          "fill": "$ink",
                          "content": "d(s, q) = 1 + (b(s) - 1) / (1 + q)",
                          "fontFamily": "$mono",
                          "fontSize": 13,
                          "fontWeight": "normal"
                        }
                      ]
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "id": "uJDAx",
                      "name": "P",
                      "fill": "#3F3E3B",
                      "textGrowth": "fixed-width",
                      "width": "fill_container",
                      "content": "The exponent nu is fitted at load to the contact probability of the cell type's Hi-C, which falls as s to the power of minus 3 nu [3]. If the fit fails, the run prints the reason and uses 0.285. The strength q of a loop is its PET count over the typical count of loops of the same span and factor. A strong loop pulls its anchors to one bead apart, figure 1.",
                      "lineHeight": 1.6,
                      "fontFamily": "$font",
                      "fontSize": 14,
                      "fontWeight": "normal"
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "frame",
                      "id": "Pdbz2",
                      "name": "Figure",
                      "width": "fill_container",
                      "layout": "vertical",
                      "gap": 6,
                      "children": [
                        {
                          "type": "frame",
                          "id": "R9Gs5",
                          "name": "Image",
                          "width": "fill_container",
                          "height": 420,
                          "fill": [
                            "#FFFFFF",
                            {
                              "type": "image",
                              "enabled": true,
                              "url": "images/method_law.png",
                              "mode": "contain"
                            }
                          ],
                          "cornerRadius": 8,
                          "stroke": "$line",
                          "strokeWidth": 1
                        },
                        {
                          "type": "frame",
                          "id": "x4iLoD",
                          "name": "Caption",
                          "width": "fill_container",
                          "gap": 6,
                          "children": [
                            {
                              "type": "text",
                              "id": "tS1gO",
                              "name": "K",
                              "fill": "$ink",
                              "content": "Figure 1",
                              "fontFamily": "$font",
                              "fontSize": 12,
                              "fontWeight": "700"
                            },
                            {
                              "type": "text",
                              "id": "A9I6ug",
                              "name": "T",
                              "fill": "$muted",
                              "textGrowth": "fixed-width",
                              "width": "fill_container",
                              "content": "Target distance against genomic separation, with nu = 0.34 as measured on GM12878 Hi-C.",
                              "lineHeight": 1.4,
                              "fontFamily": "$font",
                              "fontSize": 12,
                              "fontWeight": "normal"
                            }
                          ]
                        }
                      ]
                    }
                  ]
                },
                {
                  "type": "frame",
                  "id": "WQInI",
                  "name": "Anchor phase",
                  "width": 820,
                  "layout": "vertical",
                  "gap": 12,
                  "children": [
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "id": "BacLV",
                      "name": "Heading",
                      "fill": "$ink",
                      "content": "3. Anchor phase",
                      "fontFamily": "$font",
                      "fontSize": 18,
                      "fontWeight": "700"
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "text",
                      "id": "a0nSwV",
                      "name": "P",
                      "fill": "#3F3E3B",
                      "textGrowth": "fixed-width",
                      "width": "fill_container",
                      "content": "All anchors of a chromosome are placed in one fit. The start is a Hilbert curve [4] scaled to b(s), as in MultiMM [5]. L-BFGS-B [6, 7] then minimises the sum of the terms below. Ten annealing runs from perturbed starts end within one percent of the same energy, and L-BFGS-B reaches that minimum. The GPU evaluates the energy and its gradient, the optimiser runs on the CPU, and the fit stops when the relative improvement per iteration drops below 1e-6.",
                      "lineHeight": 1.6,
                      "fontFamily": "$font",
                      "fontSize": 14,
                      "fontWeight": "normal"
                    },
                    {
                      "type": "frame",
                      "id": "Q4osc",
